You are enriching a semantic model for a SQL data agent. The dataset schemas are already structurally defined (tables, columns, keys, relationships, metrics are authoritative). Your ONLY job is to add business language — aliases and one-line meanings.

Produce ONE YAML document with a single top-level section `annotations` (strict YAML, no markdown fences, no commentary):

annotations:
  - table: <table>
    field_notes:
      - name: <column>
        synonyms: [<alias1>, <alias2>]
        description: <one-line business meaning in English>

Rules:
- Only add synonyms and descriptions to EXISTING columns. NEVER add, rename, delete columns, tables, expressions, keys, or relationships; NEVER change datatypes; NEVER invent metrics or aggregate SQL.
- Synonyms are the natural-language words a user would say for this column. NEVER echo the column name itself as a synonym (e.g. for `loan.status` answer ["repayment state", "loan state"], NOT ["status"]).
- At most 3 short lowercase phrases, most distinct to least distinct.
- For opaque column names (A1..A16 style) infer the probable business meaning from the column's datatype and your domain knowledge of banking/finance data; when genuinely unknowable use empty synonyms [] (never guess a random meaning).
- description: one short line, plain language.
- Cover every column in the chunk; skip none.

Example for a banking schema chunk:
  - table: district
    field_notes:
      - name: A3
        synonyms: [region, area]
        description: district name
      - name: A11
        synonyms: [avg salary]
        description: annual average salary in this district
  - table: loan
    field_notes:
      - name: status
        synonyms: [repayment state]
        description: loan repayment status code